 ### How is the posted pay read?
 
-From the posting itself, or from the careers site's own pay field where there is one. Only base pay counts: a range that includes bonus, commission or equity is shown as such or left out. When a posting gives a range per level (Microsoft's "IC4" and "IC5", NVIDIA's "Level 4" and "Level 5", the company's own level names), the drawer lists each level's range under **Pay by level**, the card says "· 2 levels", and the headline range runs from the lowest to the highest. When it gives a range for each place (Amazon's pay table, Apple's and Microsoft's ranges for some areas), the drawer lists each place's own range under **Pay by place** and the card says "· N places". A posting that states only a minimum shows it as "from €46K"; an hourly rate that includes the highest shift differential says so ("Incl. top shift pay"), with the stated base beside it when the posting gives one; where a posting states a yearly figure (Austria's minimum, paid in 14 instalments) that figure is used, and the drawer says "Stated as a yearly figure" and quotes the posting. An internship's pay per hour, week or month is shown as posted, also when the posting doesn't say how long the internship lasts; it is never turned into a yearly salary, and a converted figure ("≈ $6,025 a month") is converted at the same interval. The headline median (the stats line over the chart, and Insights' median and middle half) compares one country's pay, as the chart's rows do: when the roles in view are paid in more than one currency, it is the median of the main one ("base pay median (US)"), and the rest are counted, not pooled. Part-time hourly pay is also shown as a full-time year of 2,080 hours, for comparison only.
+From the posting itself, or from the careers site's own pay field where there is one. Only base pay counts: a range that includes bonus, commission or equity is shown as such or left out. When a posting gives a range per level (Microsoft's "IC4" and "IC5", NVIDIA's "Level 4" and "Level 5", the company's own level names), the drawer lists each level's range under **Pay by level**, the card says "· 2 levels", and the headline range runs from the lowest to the highest. When it gives a range for each place (Amazon's pay table, Apple's and Microsoft's ranges for some areas), the drawer lists each place's own range under **Pay by place** and the card says "· N places". A posting that states only a minimum shows it as "from €46K"; an hourly rate that includes the highest shift differential says so ("Incl. top shift pay"), with the stated base beside it when the posting gives one; where a posting states a yearly figure (Austria's minimum, paid in 14 instalments) that figure is used, and the drawer says "Stated as a yearly figure" and quotes the posting. An internship's pay per hour, week or month is shown as posted, also when the posting doesn't say how long the internship lasts; it is never turned into a yearly salary, and a converted figure ("≈ $6,025 a month") is converted at the same interval. The headline median (the stats line over the chart, and Insights' median and middle half) compares one country's pay, as the chart's rows do: when the roles in view are paid in more than one currency, it is the median of the main one ("base pay median (US)"), and the rest are counted, not pooled. The median is written in the currency of the pay it is of, as the cards show pay: Amazon's interns in Vancouver read "CA$101K", Google's Warsaw roles "PLN …", never a dollar figure converted from them; the map's pins and Insights' tiles say the same figure. Part-time hourly pay is also shown as a full-time year of 2,080 hours, for comparison only; when every role in view is paid that way, the median is the hourly rate as posted ("$28.41/hour posted pay median"), and when only some are it says "a year if full-time". The pay filter compares each role's posted range with yours: a posted minimum ("from €31,100") states no top, so a minimum pay filter never hides it, and pay stated "up to" one figure is never hidden by a maximum; the filter's note counts every role it hides, and why.
 
 ### How are levels read?
 
-Every role gets one of seven levels: Intern, Entry, Mid, Senior, Staff+ (staff, principal and above), Manager, and Director+ (director, head of, vice president and above). Most come from the title's own words ("Senior", "Principal", "Director", "Intern"). Where a company numbers its levels, its own ladder decides: Microsoft IC1–IC2 are Entry, IC3 Mid, IC4 Senior and IC5 and up Staff+, and M1–M5 Manager (M6 and up Director+); NVIDIA Level 1 is Entry, 2 Mid, 3 to 5 Senior and 6 and up Staff+; Google's numerals I–II are Entry, III Mid, IV Senior and V and up Staff+; Amazon's I is Entry, II Mid, III and IV Senior and V and up Staff+; L-levels such as Netflix's are Entry to L3, L4 Mid, L5 Senior and L6 and up Staff+; Meta's "(Technical Leadership)" roles are Staff+. A title with no level word shows **Level not stated**, and comparisons "at Senior level" use only postings that state one. Because the ladders differ, the same numeral can mean different levels: "III" is Mid at Google but Senior at Amazon, so the Mid chip's tip gives each board's own example.
